// Three specs are tracked against this repo, and they answer different
// questions. Keep them apart in your head:
//
//   dash   — "what observable behavior did the translated Dash source have?"
//            Derived FROM the pinned C oracle. Source signatures remain as
//            historical prose, while only behavioral rules constrain Rust.
//            They do not outrank POSIX or explicit nsh compatibility rules.
//
//   posix  — "does the shell conform to POSIX.1-2024?"
//            Derived FROM the standard, so a rule here describes what
//            the standard REQUIRES. 1,130 rules cover XCU chapters 1
//            and 2, the `sh` page, and every intrinsic built-in. See
//            posix/README.md.
//
//   nsh    — "what does the Rust library promise its embedder?"
//            These rules govern isolation and ownership properties that
//            neither the dash source nor the POSIX shell language can
//            express.
//
// These will disagree, and that is the point: dash deviates from POSIX
// in places. An uncovered POSIX rule means "not established". Once a
// conflict is established, POSIX or an explicit nsh compatibility rule
// owns the shipped behavior; the dash rule remains provenance for the
// old observation rather than authority to preserve a defect.
//
// nspec keys coverage on the BARE rule id — namespace and verb are
// dropped — so an id used by both corpora would silently merge into one
// rule. There are currently 0 such collisions, but the prefixes
// `expand.`, `redir.` and `shell.` appear in both, so check before
// adding rules under those.
//
// Two more things decide what a scope actually sweeps:
//
//   * `*` in an include glob CROSSES `/`. `fuzz/*.rs` reaches
//     `fuzz/fuzz_targets/parse.rs`, and — the reason this is written down
//     — `tests/*.sh` reaches the 158 vendored Oils cases under
//     `tests/surveys/oils/spec/`, which are upstream's files and are not
//     this repository's to annotate. Name a directory that has no
//     surprising subtree, or name the files.
//   * A rule reference is a CLAIM only in bare form. `// [spec:ns:req:id]`
//     is indexed; `` `[spec:ns:req:id]` `` in a sentence is prose and is
//     not. Every citation in `fuzz/` and `tests/harness/` was written in
//     the prose form, so widening a scope over them indexes nothing until
//     a bare line is added beside the prose — which is the shape
//     `crates/nsh-cli/tests/bash_both_streams_redirection.rs` already
//     uses.
specs (
    {
        name dash,
        include (docs/spec/port/**/*.md),
        source_url "https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/utils/dash/dash.git",
        impls (
            {name target-impl, languages (@rust), include (crates/**/*.rs), exclude (target/**), mode @both}
        )
    }
    {
        name posix,
        include (posix/docs/spec/**/*.md),
        source_url "https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/",
        impls (
            // Conformance is a property of the shell we ship, so it is
            // claimed on the Rust implementation. The downloaded C oracle is
            // not a second indexed implementation and carries no repository
            // annotations.
            // Python files are verification sites only: the shipped shell
            // remains the Rust implementation, while the rule-driven harness
            // carries `[spec:posix:.../test]` evidence.
            {
                name shell
                languages (@rust @python)
                include (crates/**/*.rs posix/harness/**/*.py)
                exclude (target/**)
                mode @both
            }
        )
    }
    {
        name nsh,
        include (docs/spec/nsh/**/*.md),
        impls (
            {
                name nsh-library
                languages (@rust)
                include (crates/**/*.rs)
                exclude (target/**)
                mode @both
            }
            // `docs/spec/nsh/verification.md` is a family of rules whose
            // subject is this project's own checks, and those checks are
            // written in three languages. A single `crates/**/*.rs` scope
            // therefore settles coverage by the directory a check happens
            // to live in: `oracle.recording-carries-its-age` is satisfied
            // half in `nsh-survey` and half in `fuzz/corpus.sh`, and only
            // one half can be claimed. A file in no impl is also beyond
            // `nspec unmapped`, which cannot report a file it does not
            // know exists.
            //
            // Scoped to the checks and the harness that runs them, never
            // to `tests/surveys/`: that tree is imported from Oils and
            // upstream's cases carry upstream's meaning.
            {
                name nsh-harness
                languages (@rust @bash @python)
                include (
                    fuzz/*.rs
                    fuzz/*.sh
                    tests/harness/*
                    tests/build-locales.sh
                    tests/build-reference.sh
                    scripts/*
                )
                exclude (target/** fuzz/target/**)
                mode @both
            }
        )
    }
)
